  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nRecent water vapor imagery shows amplified flow aloft with upper \nridge over the High Plains and upper troughing along the West Coast \nand Ohio Valley with Canadian high pressure ridge at the surface \nover the Missouri/Mississippi Valley resulting in a cool and dry \nlower and middle troposphere. This pattern only slowly changes \nthrough early Sunday, with the main difference being upper level \nenergy making its way east from southern California into the Four \nCorners region Saturday and east-southeast while weakening Sunday. \nPrecipitation from this energy remains focused well to the south but \nthere remains a small chance for very light precipitation to graze \nportions of north-central, central, and east-central Kansas late \nSaturday night into Sunday. Expect any precipitation to fall as rain \nwith wetter solutions of the model suite also on the warmer side of \nthe envelope. \n\nNext week the pattern shifts to a broad southwest flow regime as \nupper troughing to the east moves off. Precipitation chances return \naround Thursday when shortwave energy may move into the region as \nlow-level flow brings warmer and more moist air into the area. Any \nprecipitation looks to stay as showers with no significant cold air \nintrusions in play. \n\nTemperatures will be cool this weekend into Monday, initially thanks \nto the Canadian high pressure but also with cloud associated with \nthe southern wave this weekend. Southerly flow kicks in Monday but \nthere are some suggestions of stratus accompanying it. Warmer \ntemperatures should dominate the bulk of next week barring \npersistent daytime precipitation.",
